Fix: TCP_NODELAY never enabled on keep-alive FastCGI connections (non-Windows)

Upstream bug in main/fastcgi.c. `req->tcp` is assigned only under `#ifdef _WIN32`
in fcgi_init_request(), but fcgi_read_request() uses it unconditionally to decide
whether to set TCP_NODELAY on a FCGI_KEEP_CONN connection. On every other platform
the field stays 0 from calloc(), so TCP_NODELAY is never set and a response larger
than the 8 KB output buffer (several write() calls, last one small) hits
Nagle + delayed ACK: ~40 ms instead of ~80 us per request against a client that
does not ACK promptly. Reproduction and proposed upstream report:
patches/0002-upstream-report.md

Fix: derive `req->tcp` from the peer address family right after accept().

Czeka na: zgloszenie do php/php-src (jeszcze nie wyslane, tekst w
0002-upstream-report.md). Znika w dniu, w ktorym upstream to zmerguje.
Sprawdzone na: PHP-8.3, PHP-8.4, PHP-8.5, master (patrz patches/README.md).

diff --git a/main/fastcgi.c b/main/fastcgi.c
index 6cdf023334a..d5eb6289530 100644
--- a/main/fastcgi.c
+++ b/main/fastcgi.c
@@ -1395,6 +1395,13 @@ int fcgi_accept_request(fcgi_request *req)
 					req->fd = accept(listen_socket, (struct sockaddr *)&sa, &len);
 					FCGI_UNLOCK(req->listen_socket);
 
+#ifndef _WIN32
+					/* On Windows req->tcp is set once in fcgi_init_request() (pipe vs socket).
+					 * Elsewhere it was never set at all, so TCP_NODELAY was never enabled. */
+					if (req->fd >= 0) {
+						req->tcp = (sa.sa.sa_family != AF_UNIX);
+					}
+#endif
 					client_sa = sa;
 					if (req->fd >= 0 && !fcgi_is_allowed()) {
 						fcgi_log(FCGI_ERROR, "Connection disallowed: IP address '%s' has been dropped.", fcgi_get_last_client_ip());
